Z||000000||ur2biteme||09-15-2000||11:37 PM||||i'm looking for a little info that i couldn't find in the search threads.  some techincal stuff from the homebrew instalations as well as other things.<P>how much total price?  homebrew?  kit?  turnkey?<P>how much difference in feel between the sc and without?<P>did the 3800 come with a manual?  if not, how did you get everything to work?  has anyone taken the time to bolt a manual to this?<P>i've allready talked to big o about his, but he has the 4 speed auto, so i'm trying to find out more info about that.  this is later down the line and i'm not against kits, so any recomendations.  <P>tia    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>UR2BITEME<BR>'87 econo coupe<BR>169k and a bad fender  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by ur2biteme (edited 09-15-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||terryk||09-16-2000||01:08 AM||||In proces....<P><BR>&gt;how much total price? <P>About $1200 for the engine and trans (52k) and about $500-700 in misc parts. Homebrew. No kludges.<P>&gt;how much difference in feel between the sc and without?<P>Once you've been blown, you never go back!<P>&gt;did the 3800 come with a manual? if not, how did you get everything to work? <P>Never did.<P>&gt;has anyone taken the time to bolt a manual to this?<P>I'm using the 4T60E. There are custom flywheels available. Expect the performance to have little gremlins if you do.<P>&gt;i've allready talked to big o about his, but he has the 4 speed auto, so i'm trying to find out more info about that. this is later down the line and i'm not against kits, so any recomendations. <P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 09-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||ur2biteme||09-17-2000||12:00 AM||||anyone else?||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||09-17-2000||12:13 AM||||Call Chris West at West Coast Fiero's. He can give you a good run-down of the issues. I know he sells kits, but he's pretty open about discussing the issues without going into salesman mode.<P>The only other 5spd 3800 I know about got totalled.<P>Terry<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 09-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||rockcrawl||09-17-2000||07:51 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.americancustomeng.com/3800-V6.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.americancustomeng.com/3800-V6.htm</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://home.earthlink.net/~chriswest/Engines/Engines.html" TARGET=_blank>http://home.earthlink.net/~chriswest/Engines/Engines.html</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.terranexus.com/fieroman/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.terranexus.com/fieroman/</A> <P>Thats all the info I could find before doing my swap. I used an '87 3.8, <BR>bolted to a Fiero muncie 4 spd. Stock 12,000 mile old Sachs clutch, no slipping. 2.8 flywheel, plugged and re-drilled, with a bushing pressed in the center ($20 for machine work). No ECM problems, I think the later OBD-II systems are the ones that have stalling problems between shifts when used with a manual trans. Besides the increase in power, the most noticable change is the throttle response and the smoothness of the engine. It revs much quicker than the 2.8 and the RPM drops down quickly between shifts. Very cool to drive!<P>Total cost was not more than $500 including the engine! (which I got free), but not including my labor. A lot of my expenses were for replacing things like motor mounts and CV boots that were worn out.<P>More info at my site <A HREF="http://www.fortunecity.com/silverstone/impala/403/3800fiero.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fortunecity.com/silverstone/impala/403/3800fiero.html</A> <P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||7
Z||000005||pollock||09-18-2000||09:47 AM||||Hey rockcrawl;<P>Did you just weld the old flywheel holes shut or weld little plugs in them?  <P>How did you get the proper flywheel balance?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||rockcrawl||09-18-2000||06:36 PM||||The 87 3800 has only six flywheel bolts like the Fiero. The 3.8 uses bigger bolts and has one offset hole instead of dowel pins like the fiero. I cut the center out of the stock 3.8 flexplate and layed it over the fiero flywheel to drill the holes in the correct places. I had a temporary plug made to fit in the center hole to keep everything lined up. I clamped it down and drilled the holes in a drill press using an end mill bit. The new holes covered all of the old ones with the exception of a few tiny places on two of the holes, which don't matter because of the large heads on the bolts. I used bolts by Mr.Gasket off the shelf at Auto Zone for $6. The counterweight of the 3.8 and 2.8 are at the same degree if you line up the holes properly, but not quite the same weight as I do have a slightly noticable vibration. I should have had the new flywheel balanced to match the old flexplate, but I figured it was close enough, and it is. From 88-on, the 3800 has 8 crank bolts, and they need to be plugged before drilling new holes. The plugs should be a tight press-fit, and the surface should be machined or grond smooth. Then the new holes can be drilled using the same method above, while keeping the counterweight as close as possible, and have the wheel balanced when done. Some of the holes will go partially through the plugs, but they will remain tight and not fall out. Once the bolts are installed they will be locked in even more. One of the websites I listed above has the specs for the weight. I have never done an 8 bolt flywheel, but I'v heard from others who have done it succesfully. I'll probably be doing one for my 4.9 conversion if I decide to go manual with it. I don't remember why, but someone told me not to weld the holes, possibly because it would weaken the flywheel. <P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
